      "13": { k: "What can be learnt",
        b: "Two things: **a task** (learn to *do* something, e.g. a policy: reinforcement learning, not in this course) or **a model** (a description of the relation among variables/signals). This course is about learning **models**." },
      "14": { k: "Human vs machine learning", b: "We focus on **automated (machine) learning**: algorithms that build models from data." },
      "15": { k: "Why machines that learn",
        b: "- Tasks **too complex to program** by hand (computer vision: we recognise objects but cannot write the rules).\n- Tasks **too time-consuming** for humans (search engines).\n- **Adaptivity** and faster development." },
      "16-21": { k: "Applications",
        b: "Examples to remember, with the type of problem:\n\n- **Spam filtering**: binary classification, noisy labels, spammers adapt.\n- **Movie rating**: regression/prediction with very sparse data.\n- **Handwritten digits**: multi-class classification (comes back in PCA, Lecture 10).\n- **Credit scoring**: classification from historical data (see logistic regression and the loan-default exam question).\n- **Generative AI**.",
        x: "Exams often wrap a question in a story like these (loan default, churn, machine failure). First identify: input x, output y, classification or regression?" },
      "22-24": { k: "Why it matters in automation",
        b: "Classical robots do one task in a known environment and can be *programmed*. New robots work in **unknown, changing environments**, so they must **learn from experience**. Autonomous cars: perception and a strong link with control.\n\nSlide 24 shows the three typical roles of ML in a control loop: **black-box modelling** of the plant, **software sensing** (estimating a variable you cannot measure, e.g. wheel slip) and **auto-tuning** of the controller." },
